The standard error implied by the published interval is 0.049 on the log scale. Against the margin the one-sided p-value is 0.0037, in line with the reported 0.003 (the reported value comes from the trial's own analysis, which can differ a little from a calculation on the rounded interval), so noninferiority is established. Against zero the one-sided p-value is 0.05, in line with the reported two-sided 0.09 for superiority, so superiority is not. Both statements come from the same interval. The two tests have two different nulls, and the trial can win the first and lose the second without contradiction. Notice also that the margin did the work: the upper limit 1.01 is above 1.00 and below 1.05, and a margin of 1.00 would have been a superiority trial.

The traditional report stops here: noninferiority met, superiority not met. What it does not give is the probability that the hazard ratio lies below 1.05, below 1.00, or below 0.90, nor the effect against placebo that the dulaglutide comparison implies. The probability that the hazard ratio lies below a given value is a posterior probability, which is where the Bayesian distinction lies. The effect against placebo has frequentist treatments as well, such as the synthesis approach above. Both are taken up in @sec-borrowing-ni and @sec-borrowing-chain.

## Where the traditional trial stands, and what changes next {#sec-testing-bridge}

Put the pieces together and you have the traditional single-look trial. The protocol states the endpoint, the effect to be detected, the one-sided level of 0.025 and the power. It fixes $n$ from these. The trial runs to completion. The analysis computes a test statistic and a p-value from the pre-specified method, and an interval for the effect. The decision is "success" if the p-value is at most 0.025, one-sided, and the CSR reports the interval alongside. The method is transparent, its error rates are known by construction (type I by the choice of the level, power by calculation or simulation), and it has a regulatory history of decades. It is the benchmark against which every design later in the book is judged. When there is one look, a single primary endpoint and no useful prior information, it is also hard to improve on, and the book says so where it matters (@sec-cases).

What this design does not do is answer the questions a decision maker actually has. The p-value does not give the probability that the drug works, the interval's 95% is a statement about the procedure, and the error rates describe a procedure that is run once at a fixed sample size, so they change when the trial is monitored, when several endpoints or arms are examined, or when earlier information is brought in. Each of these is a place where the traditional answer needs repair, and where a Bayesian formulation offers another route at a price that the book will itemize. The table summarizes what this chapter established and where the book goes next.

| Question | Traditional answer (this chapter) | Where it goes next |
|---|---|---|
| Is there a difference? | p-value against $H_0$: the share of reruns or shuffles at least this extreme | Evidence beyond p-values and Bayes factors (@sec-evidence) |
| How big is it? | Estimate and 95% confidence interval | Posterior and credible interval (@sec-bayes) |
| What error rates does the rule have? | Type I fixed at 0.025 one-sided; power by formula | Power and sample size (@sec-power); operating characteristics by simulation (@sec-simulation) |
| What if we look more than once? | The p-value is no longer calibrated | Group sequential designs (@sec-sequential) |
| Is the new drug not much worse than an active control? | One-sided test against a margin (@sec-testing-ni) | Bayesian noninferiority and margin synthesis (@sec-borrowing-ni) |
| What justifies the test? | Randomization (shuffling labels) | Randomization and the design (@sec-randomization) |
| What does the regulator expect? | Pre-specified test, side, level, interval (E9 section 5.5) | The regulatory map (@sec-regulatory) |

Where do the two approaches agree numerically? In a simple setting with a flat prior, the Bayesian posterior probability that the drug is better than control is close to one minus the one-sided p-value. For our continuous trial this is a statement about 0.995, and for the binary trial about 0.986. The agreement is not a coincidence, and it tells us that the Bayesian bar and the traditional bar coincide when there is a single look and no prior information. They diverge once the design is made more flexible, and the point of the book is to show how and by how much.

