## In practice {#sec-testing-practice}

Two real trials show the machinery of this chapter at work. One demonstrated a mortality benefit; the other remained inconclusive despite an estimate compatible with substantial benefit.

### RECOVERY: a two-group comparison, a rate ratio and an interval

The RECOVERY platform trial randomized hospitalized patients with Covid-19 to usual care or to one of several treatments. Its dexamethasone comparison is, statistically, our trial: two groups, a binary outcome (death within 28 days) and a decision about whether the gap is more than chance [@recovery_dexamethasone_2021]. Of 2104 patients assigned to dexamethasone, 482 (22.9%) died, against 1110 of 4321 (25.7%) on usual care, a difference of 2.8 percentage points. The headline estimate was the age-adjusted rate ratio 0.83, with 95% confidence interval 0.75 to 0.93 and P < 0.001. The analysis was frequentist: no prior and no posterior probability, only a p-value and an interval computed from the repetition-of-the-trial logic of this chapter.

Two features connect it to what we built. The interval is as informative as the p-value: the data are compatible with a relative reduction in mortality of 7% to 25%, which is the range a clinician needs. And the size of the trial, thousands of patients rather than our 100 per arm, is what makes a 2.8 point gap decisive; the same gap in a trial of 100 per arm would be buried in a standard error of about 0.06. The lesson for this chapter: a clean randomized two-group comparison with a pre-specified analysis needs no more than the tools above, and when it is large enough the traditional answer is a good one. The design side of the same trial (concealed central randomization, open label, an objective endpoint) is in @sec-randomization-practice, and RECOVERY returns as a platform in @sec-platform.

### ANDROMEDA-SHOCK: what "not significant" does and does not mean

ANDROMEDA-SHOCK randomized 424 patients with septic shock in 28 intensive care units to resuscitation targeting capillary refill time (212 patients) or serum lactate (212) [@hernandez2019]. The primary outcome was 28-day mortality: 74 deaths (34.9%) against 92 (43.4%), a risk difference of -8.5 points (95% CI -18.2 to 1.2) and a hazard ratio of 0.75 (95% CI 0.55 to 1.02), P = .06. On the convention of this chapter, a two-sided P above 0.05 is a miss: the interval contains no effect.

That verdict is correct, and it is not the same as "capillary refill targeting does nothing". The same interval contains a 45% relative reduction in the hazard of death and a 2% increase. A p-value of .06 says the data are not very surprising if there is no effect, and, as the definition above warns, they may be just as unsurprising under a substantial benefit. The trial was inconclusive between two clinically different possibilities, and the p-value alone cannot say which. A post hoc Bayesian reanalysis of the same trial is in @sec-reanalyses. It shows why later chapters ask for a probability statement about the effect, which a p-value cannot give. The lesson: report the estimate and the interval, read a large p-value as "inconclusive" unless the interval excludes effects that matter, and decide in the protocol what will count as success. @sec-evidence takes this example further.

## Key points {.unnumbered}

- A trial compares groups of patients who vary, so a difference in averages is not yet evidence. The sampling distribution (the observed difference over many imagined reruns) shows how much a difference can move by chance alone. Its width is the standard error, which shrinks like $1/\sqrt{n}$.
- The randomization test needs only the coin that allocated the patients: pool the outcomes, shuffle the labels and count how often chance matches the observed gap. The z test is the same idea with a bell curve in place of shuffling, and it is an approximation that is least reliable for binary outcomes at moderate $n$ close to the threshold.
- The p-value is the probability, under the null and over repetitions of the design, of a result at least as extreme as the one observed. It is not the probability that the null is true, it depends on the planned analyses and stopping rule, and it says nothing about the size or importance of the effect.
- A confidence interval reports the effects that the data do not rule out. The 95% refers to the long-run record of the method. The interval and the test are the same calculation: the interval is the set of null values a test would not reject. ICH E9 section 5.5 asks for intervals alongside tests.
- The type I error rate $\alpha$ is a property of the rule under the null world, and power $1 - \beta$ is a property under a stated alternative. At fixed information, lowering $\alpha$ generally increases $\beta$ and reduces power. More information can reduce both error probabilities. In our example, 100 patients per arm give 60% power for 30% against 45%.
- Regulators set the confirmatory bar at a one-sided 0.025 (half of a conventional two-sided 0.05), prefer that the one-sided choice be justified before the trial (ICH E9 section 5.5), and the 2026 FDA Bayesian draft guidance uses the same bar for success criteria. For confirmatory adaptive designs without borrowing, this book uses calibration to one-sided 0.025. FDA's draft also describes direct posterior interpretation and decision-theoretic or benefit-risk criteria (section IV.A).

