1. In @fig-testing-rerun, the no-effect histogram has about 1.0% of reruns at or above the observed difference, yet the observed trial is called significant at one-sided 0.025. Explain, in terms of reruns, why 1% of a null world and "the probability that the drug works" are different things. What additional information would you need to convert one into the other?
2. The binary trial has a normal-approximation one-sided p-value of 0.014 and a randomization p-value of 0.021. Both clear 0.025, but suppose they had been 0.024 and 0.031. Which should the statistical analysis plan have named, when, and who decides? What does that imply for how close to the threshold a "pass" can be trusted?
3. A confidence interval of 0.017 to 0.283 and a p-value of 0.014 come from the same trial. A sponsor says the trial proved the drug works. A clinician says it may help only 2 patients in 100. Which statement does the interval support, and which does the p-value support? How should the CSR present both?
4. A colleague proposes switching from a two-sided 0.05 to a one-sided 0.05 because "the drug can only help". Using E9 section 5.5 and the duality between tests and intervals, explain why this is not a harmless change.
5. Power of 60% was enough for our observed trial to succeed. Why is it still a poor design? Who bears the cost of the 40%?
